  "preamble": "Voicing of the voiceless postalveolar affricate [tʃ] to [dʒ], typically in weak positions: between vowels, after an unstressed vowel, or (in Turkic) after an originally long vowel. Late Middle English voiced [tʃ] in unstressed final syllables (knowleche > knowledge, -wich place names), and the Oghuz Turkic languages voiced Proto-Turkic *č intervocalically and, in Azerbaijani and Ottoman, also word-finally. In Western Armenian the Classical Armenian affricate ճ /t͡ʃ/ became [d͡ʒ] (ճաշ 'meal' [d͡ʒɑʃ]), while Eastern Armenian keeps [t͡ʃ].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Voicing, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "English",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle English knoweleche, knowleche 'knowledge' (< late Old English cnāwelǣċ)",
          "to": "knowledge /ˈnɒlɪdʒ/",
          "note": "[tʃ] voiced to [dʒ] in the unstressed final syllable."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English norþ + wīċ 'north settlement'",
          "to": "Norwich /ˈnɒrɪdʒ/ (local Norfolk pronunciation; also /ˈnɒrɪtʃ/)",
          "note": "The unstressed -wich [wiːtʃ] element shows the same voicing, also heard as a variant in Greenwich /ˈɡrɛnɪdʒ/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Turkish",
      "languageFamily": "Oghuz",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Turkic *āčïg 'bitter'",
          "to": "acı [ɑˈdʒɯ] 'bitter'",
          "note": "Intervocalic *č after an originally long vowel became [dʒ] (Old Anatolian Turkish acu); Karluk and Kipchak keep [tʃ] (Uzbek achchiq, Tatar ачы)."
        },
        {
          "from": "ağaç [ɑːtʃ] 'tree'",
          "to": "ağacı [ɑːdʒɯ] 'its tree; tree (acc.)'",
          "note": "Synchronic alternation: stem-final ç voices before a vowel-initial suffix. Ottoman Turkish had voiced ağac even in the nominative."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Azerbaijani",
      "languageFamily": "Oghuz",
      "examples": [
